public with sharing class AccountCountrySync {

    public static void syncContactCountries(List<Account> newAccounts, Map<Id, Account> oldMap) {
        // 1. Only accounts whose BillingCountry changed
        Map<Id, String> newCountryByAccount = new Map<Id, String>();
        Map<Id, String> oldCountryByAccount = new Map<Id, String>();
        Set<String> oldCountries = new Set<String>();

        for (Account acc : newAccounts) {
            Account oldAcc = oldMap == null ? null : oldMap.get(acc.Id);
            if (oldAcc == null) {
                continue;
            }
            if (acc.BillingCountry != oldAcc.BillingCountry) {
                newCountryByAccount.put(acc.Id, acc.BillingCountry);
                oldCountryByAccount.put(acc.Id, oldAcc.BillingCountry);
                if (String.isNotBlank(oldAcc.BillingCountry)) {
                    oldCountries.add(oldAcc.BillingCountry);
                }
            }
        }

        if (newCountryByAccount.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        Set<Id> accountIds = newCountryByAccount.keySet();

        // 2. One selective query: only contacts of changed accounts that could qualify
        List<Contact> candidates = [
            SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry
            FROM Contact
            WHERE AccountId IN :accountIds
            AND (MailingCountry = null OR MailingCountry = '' OR MailingCountry IN :oldCountries)
        ];

        // 3. Decide per contact in memory
        List<Contact> toUpdate = new List<Contact>();
        for (Contact c : candidates) {
            String oldCountry = oldCountryByAccount.get(c.AccountId);
            String newCountry = newCountryByAccount.get(c.AccountId);

            Boolean matches = String.isBlank(c.MailingCountry)
                || (String.isNotBlank(oldCountry) && c.MailingCountry.equalsIgnoreCase(oldCountry));

            if (matches && c.MailingCountry != newCountry) {
                toUpdate.add(new Contact(Id = c.Id, MailingCountry = newCountry));
            }
        }

        // 4. Single DML
        if (!toUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            update toUpdate;
        }
    }
}
```

The main design problem is that `favorite` is public and set by the parent, but a click must also change the displayed state. LWC forbids mutating a public property inside the component (`@lwc/lwc/no-api-reassignments`). So the component keeps a private tracked copy of the state and syncs it from the public setter, so parent changes always win.

The setter normalises the incoming value with `=== true`, so `favorite` is always a boolean. Passing `"false"` as a string from markup is not handled specially, which is the correct LWC behaviour.

The click handler reads the private state, flips it, and dispatches the event with the new value. The event is not bubbling or composed, because the parent listens directly on the component.

Both `aria-label` and `title` use a getter that returns the label of the action a click performs. `aria-pressed` needs the strings `"true"` or `"false"`, so a getter converts the boolean.

The icon is rendered as inline SVG so no extra dependency is needed. Its visible text is not hard-coded, and the SVG is `aria-hidden`. A star is drawn filled when the record is a favourite and outlined otherwise.

